<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The Living Peoples</h1><p class="ws-lede">Ten Indigenous peoples alive today: their deep pasts, colonial dispossession and recovery of rights.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/the-living-peoples">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Bantustan</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Caravan trade</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Colonial rule</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Aboriginal Australians</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Their ancestors reached Sahul 50,000 to 65,000 years ago; more than 400 peoples, each with its own language and Country, survived British colonisation from 1788.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Inuit</b>: &#8220;Descendants of the Thule people who spread east from the Bering Strait around 1000 CE, the Inuit survived forced relocation and won the land claim that created Nunavut in 1999.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Lakota people</b>: &#8220;Bison hunters of the northern Plains who with the Cheyenne defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876, they still refuse the 1980 Supreme Court award for the Black Hills.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Ten Indigenous peoples alive today: their deep pasts, colonial dispossession and recovery of rights.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/the-living-peoples. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The Living Peoples</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Bantustan</dt><dd>A Bantustan, or homeland, was a territory set aside for Black South Africans of a particular ethnic group under apartheid.</dd><dt>Caravan trade</dt><dd>Caravan trade is long-distance overland commerce carried by groups of merchants with pack animals, typically camels, travelling together for safety.</dd><dt>Colonial rule</dt><dd>Colonial rule, the government of territories and peoples by distant states as subjects rather than citizens, carried European power across the world from 1415 and shaped its borders, languages, economies and states.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
